## SECTION 5: AUDIENCE INTELLIGENCE
**who_they_are**: Two overlapping searcher types under one query — (1) nail art hobbyists/nail techs wanting a practice tool to sketch designs before committing polish to real nails, and (2) parents/kids or craft-hobbyists wanting a polish-free "paper nails" play activity. Both are DIY-minded, budget-conscious, printing at home rather than buying a kit.

**what_they_want_from_this_post**: A clear explanation of what these templates actually are, confirmation they can get one free and print it correctly, and reassurance about sizing/shape choice so it isn't wasted paper.

**what_frustrates_them**: Thin competitor pages that are just download links with no real explanation; confusion between "template" and "stencil/decal" (different products entirely); templates that print the wrong size for their actual nails; no guidance on shape selection for beginners.

**top_5_pain_points**:
1. Printing a template that turns out too big/small for real nails (confirmed: Etsy review noting sizing issue).
2. Not knowing which nail shape template to pick (round, square, almond, coffin, stiletto, squoval).
3. Confusing a blank practice template with pre-made stencils/decals.
4. Wanting a reusable version (lamination) rather than printing a fresh page every time.
5. Wanting a screen-free/polish-free kids activity but only finding professional nail-tech resources.

**exact_user_phrases**: "print this out and write down the polish names," "get my nail art sizes right," "draw out how the opposite hand should look," "safe space to perfect your designs," "screen-free rainy day activity," "print, cut, and apply," "reusable practice sheet," "laminate the PDF," "true-to-nail scale," "practice before committing to real nails."

**intent_content_signals**: Wants a direct definition early (AEO-style), practical how-to-use steps, shape/size guidance, a clear split between practice-tool use and paper-play use, and mention of reusability/lamination.

